          "question": "Got stung by a wasp an hour ago, my hand has ballooned and now my eyelids look puffy and I feel strange.",
          "answer": "**Emergency care is needed now. Call your local emergency number (for example 911, 999 or 112) immediately.** Do not wait to see if it settles.\n\nSwelling that spreads beyond the sting, puffy eyelids, and feeling strange within an hour of a sting can be signs of a serious allergic reaction, which can become anaphylaxis. Anaphylaxis can get worse quickly.\n\nThe rule-table check returned \"routine\" because no listed pattern matched. That is not a clearance, and it does not outweigh the symptoms you describe. The reference material I searched did not contain guidance on wasp stings or anaphylaxis, so I cannot cite a document for this advice.\n\n**While you wait for help:**\n- Tell the emergency operator about the wasp sting and your symptoms.\n- Do not drive yourself.\n- If you feel faint, lie down. If you are vomiting, lie on your side.\n- If a clinician has given you an emergency allergy medicine for stings, follow the instructions you were given for it.\n- Do not wait for more symptoms before calling.\n\n**Call emergency services at once if you notice any of these:**\n- Trouble breathing, wheezing, or a tight chest\n- Tight throat, hoarse voice, or trouble swallowing\n- Swelling of the lips, tongue, or face that is getting worse\n- Dizziness, fainting, or feeling like you might pass out\n- Confusion, unusual drowsiness, or a sense that something is badly wrong\n- Pale, grey, or blue skin or lips\n- A racing, weak, or irregular pulse\n- Vomiting, belly cramps, or widespread hives or itching\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
